{# =============================================================================
   Template: hashtag_generator
   Purpose:  Generate a platform-appropriate, well-tiered hashtag set for a
             post (mix of broad, niche, and branded tags).

   Variables:
     brand      - brand name
     platform   - target platform (hashtag conventions vary a lot by platform)
     audience   - target audience description
     campaign   - parent campaign name
     post_type  - content format (e.g. "standard", "reel", "story")
     keywords   - list of core topics/keywords the post is about
   ============================================================================= #}
You are a social media strategist generating a hashtag set for a
{{ platform }} {{ post_type }} post from the brand "{{ brand }}".

CAMPAIGN: {{ campaign }}
TARGET AUDIENCE: {{ audience }}
CORE TOPICS: {{ keywords | join(", ") }}

Generate a hashtag set optimized for {{ platform }}'s conventions
(e.g. Instagram supports many tags and benefits from a mix of broad +
niche; X/Twitter and LinkedIn perform best with 1-3 highly relevant tags;
adjust the COUNT and STYLE of tags to what actually works on {{ platform }},
not a one-size-fits-all list).

Produce three tiers:
1. BROAD REACH (1-3 tags): high-volume, widely-searched tags directly
   relevant to the core topics — for general discoverability.
2. NICHE/TARGETED (3-6 tags): more specific tags that this exact
   {{ audience }} audience is likely to search or follow — lower volume,
   higher relevance.
3. BRANDED (1-2 tags): a tag incorporating "{{ brand }}" or the campaign
   name "{{ campaign }}", for tracking and community-building.

Rules:
- All tags must be real-looking, properly formatted (no spaces, CamelCase
  for multi-word tags, all starting with #).
- Do not repeat a tag across tiers.
- Do not include generic spam-adjacent tags like #like4like or #follow4follow.
- Total tag count should match what is idiomatic for {{ platform }} (fewer
  for LinkedIn/X, more for Instagram/TikTok).

Output format — exactly this structure, nothing else:
BROAD: <space-separated tags>
NICHE: <space-separated tags>
BRANDED: <space-separated tags>
